  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Typical summer weather through this evening with \nscattered thunderstorms near the coast, then becoming mostly dry \novernight.\n\nIsolated to scattered thunderstorms have developed across SE VA, NE \nNC, and the Eastern Shore. The initial line that developed along a \nweak surface boundary across NE NC appeared to be moving, however, \nthe motion of storms has since become very slow to nearly \nstationary. The more stationary storms have likely developed along a \nsea-breeze boundary, with no storms developing inland thus far. This \nhas lead to some flash-flooding across the VA Beach area, as well as \nareas near Melfa, VA on the Eastern Shore (radar estimated rainfall \nhas reached nearly 4\" of rain over the past hour or so in this \narea). Additionally, some of these storms have been able to develop \nimpressive cores that has led to the issuance of a few Severe \nThunderstorm Warnings. Current environmental analysis shows decent \nbulk shear values ranging between 30-40 kt, ML CAPE values of 1,000-\n2,000J/kg, and strong ridging over the central/southern CONUS and \ntroughing over New England and offshore keeping our area within a NW \nmid/upper low regime. With this kind of environment, additional \nsevere storms and localized flash flooding are possible along the \ncoast. Hi-res guidance is not excited about additional convection \ndeveloping farther inland this evening, and rain chances remain very \nlow west of I-95. As the afternoon convection wanes, rain chances \nwill dwindle to 10% or less through tomorrow morning. With light \nwinds forecast tonight, areas that saw rain may see some patchy fog. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread showers and thunderstorms expected \nTuesday afternoon and evening with better potential for severe \nweather. The main threat is damaging wind gusts with large hail also \npossible.\n\nAn anomalously strong upper trough and sfc low are progged to deepen \nover the Northeast CONUS. In fact, the low could deepen to 993mb \nwith -2 to -3 sigma height anomalies over the northern Mid-\nAtlantic/Northeast. The synoptic features are impressive especially \nconsidering it is late July. There will be a belt of 40-50 kt 500mb \nwinds on the SW side of the trough that may move over the local area \nduring the afternoon. At the same time, the low will drag a cold \nfront toward the area. Ahead of any developing convection, \ntemperatures will be able to climb into the upper 80s in most area \nand help increase the instability across the area. Forecast \nsoundings currently show solid mid level lapse rates of 6.5-7.0 \ndegrees, and MLCAPE values of 2000-3000J/kg. With very good \nconvective parameters, combined with the NW flow aloft, there could \nbe a robust damaging wind and hail threat for tomorrow. Isolated \nbrief tornadoes cannot be ruled out tomorrow, especially closer to \nthe coast where the winds will be more backed. SPC has maintained a \nSlight Risk (level 2/5) for the entire area, but has expanded the \n30% wind contour all the way to the coast and upped the tornado \nthreat slightly to account for the better environment near the \ncoast. The main bust potential will be any upstream MCS activity \novernight into tomorrow morning that could potentially contaminate \nour environment and lead to a lower than anticipated severe threat \ntomorrow. Storms will be moving quickly, which will act to limit \nflooding, but cannot rule out localized flash-flooding, mainly in \nurban/flood prone areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Isolated showers and storms are possible on \nWednesday, with mainly dry weather to end the week. Temperatures \ndrop to slightly below average late this week.\n\nThere is some uncertainty in regards to rain chances in the wake of \nthe cold front on Wednesday. With the closer proximity of the upper \nlow, there cold be some showers on Wednesday. Blended guidance still \nhas low PoPs, but have decided to include slight chance PoPs across \nthe northern portion of the forecast area. This may need to be \nexpanded pending the location of the upper low. Thursday through \nFriday looks mostly dry, with near to slightly below average \ntemperatures, as LLVL flow will continue to be out of the NW.",
